
{"id":"82361e0c-cba0-4f5c-bf15-eb16ee1cc573","object":"chat.completion","created":1785012587,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"```json\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    from collections import Counter\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    global_sym_count = Counter()\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        global_sym_count[n['symbol']] += 1\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_node_ids = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == 'root' or not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols and global_sym_count[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        global_sym_count[sym] -= 1\\n        removed_node_ids.append(nid)\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_node_ids, 'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids}\"\n}\n```","reasoning_content":"We need to write the solve(case) function for SE-PE-01. The case payload contains a tree structure with nodes, each having id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. We need to perform grammar-guided node reduction: remove nodes that are removable, but preserve required symbols and ensure tree validity. The description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But the specifics: We need to return removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted). The algorithm: consider deeper nodes before shallower ones, never remove root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nInterpretation: This is like a \"minimum parser reduction\" problem. We have a tree, we can remove nodes that are removable (removable=true) as long as we don't lose required symbols. Also we cannot remove a node that has retained children (because that would orphan them? Or because if we remove a node, its children would be lost? The spec: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if we decide to keep a child, we cannot remove its parent. So it's a bottom-up prune: we examine nodes, and if a node is removable and removing it does not cause loss of any required symbol, we can remove it. But we must process deeper first (post-order). Also tie break by node ID.\n\nThe \"grammar-valid deletion\" might imply that we cannot remove nodes that would cause symbol gaps? But we have required_symbols which must be present in the retained tree. So we need to ensure after removal, the remaining nodes still collectively include all required symbols.\n\nAdditionally, \"registered_candidate_order\" suggests we consider candidates in a specific order: deeper nodes first, then shallower, and within same depth, order by node ID (ascending? It says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\" and \"sorted retained IDs\" - sorted ascending presumably.\n\nWe need to return removed_node_ids (list of IDs in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of IDs of nodes that remain after all removals). Also the root is always retained (not removable). The required_symbols list: the symbols that must be present in the tree after reduction. For example, \"root\" and \"s1\". So if we remove a node with symbol \"s1\", we need to ensure another node with that symbol remains. Or the required_symbols are the set of symbols that must be represented in the final tree. So we cannot remove all nodes that have a required symbol.\n\nWe must also consider that removing a node also removes its subtree (since if parent removed, children are gone). But the spec says \"never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That implies if we decide to retain a child, we cannot remove its parent. So we must process bottom-up: for each node, before deciding to remove, check if any of its descendants are retained (not removed). If any descendant is retained, the node cannot be removed. Also, the node must be removable flag true. Also, after removing the node (and its subtree), the set of retained symbols must still cover required_symbols. But note that if we remove a node, its subtree is gone, so any symbols from that subtree disappear. So we need to ensure that the required symbols are still present in the tree after removal.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n1. Build a mapping from node id to node object. Also build children mapping: for each node, list its children (by parent field). We need to handle depth order processing.\n\n2. Determine the order to consider nodes: sort nodes by depth descending (deeper first), then by node id ascending (tie break). But also ensure we process children before parents? Actually depth descending ensures we process deeper nodes before shallower. But within same depth, node id ascending. However, note that if we remove a node, we should not later consider its children because they are already gone? But since we process deeper first, we will have already processed children. So we can collect a list of candidate nodes for removal. But we must also track which nodes are retained/removed dynamically.\n\nBecause the decision for a parent depends on whether any of its children are retained. Since we process children first, we can know which children are retained.\n\n3. Initialize a set of removed_node_ids = empty, retained_node_ids = all node ids initially (or we can start with all nodes as retained, then remove as we go). But careful: the decision to remove a node is based on current retained set. So we can start with a set 'retained' containing all nodes. Also maintain a set 'removed' empty.\n\n4. For each node in the sorted order (depth descending, then id ascending):\n   - Skip if node is root (id 'root')? The spec says never remove root. Also skip if node is not removable.\n   - Check if any descendant of this node is retained. But we don't want O(n^2). Instead, we can maintain a 'descendant_retained' flag per node. Since we process children first, we can propagate up: after processing children, we know if any child is retained. For a node, if any child is retained, then this node cannot be removed (because it has retained children). Also if any descendant in its subtree is retained, that implies at least one child is retained (since retained children propagate). So we can just check if the node has any retained child. But what about deeper descendants? If a child is removed, but grandchild retained? That can't happen because if grandchild is retained, then child must be retained (since child is ancestor of retained node, and we never remove a node with retained children). So indeed, if any descendant is retained, then the immediate parent must be retained as well. So checking for any retained child is sufficient. So we can maintain a 'has_retained_child' per node that is true if any direct child is retained. But we need to update this as we remove nodes. When we decide to remove a node, we also need to update parent's retained child status? Actually when we remove a node, we are removing it and its entire subtree. So its children are no longer present (they are removed). So its parent loses that child. So we need to propagate that the parent's retained child flag may become false if that was the only retained child. However, since we process in bottom-up order, by the time we consider a node, we have already processed its children. And if we decide to remove the node, we haven't yet considered its parent? Actually depth descending ensures we process children before parents. So when we consider a node, all its descendants are already processed. But if we decide to remove the node, we need to update the parent's child status later? However, the parent hasn't been processed yet. We could store for each node a list of children. Then when we remove a node, we can update parent's 'retained_children_count' or something. But simpler: we can perform the removal decision in a single pass, and after we decide to remove a node, we also remove all its descendants from the retained set. But we must also update parent's 'has_retained_child' by checking if any other child remains retained after removal. But since we are processing in order, by the time we consider the parent, we have already removed some of its children. So we need to know which children are currently retained. So we can maintain a set 'retained' that we update as we remove nodes. Then for a candidate node, we check if any of its children are in 'retained'. If yes, cannot remove. Also check if the node is removable. Also check required_symbols: after tentative removal (including its subtree), would all required symbols still be present? That is, compute the set of symbols (from nodes in retained minus the node and its subtree? Actually we need to check before removing whether the required symbols are still represented by the retained nodes after removal. So we need the current retained set (including this node and its subtree). Then consider removing this node and its subtree: compute new retained set (remove all nodes in that subtree). Then check if the set of symbols in new retained set still contains all required_symbols. If yes, proceed to remove.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build a tree structure: node objects, children list, also map from node id to node.\n- Build a set of all node ids.\n- We need to traverse to get all descendants of a node quickly. Since we process bottom-up, we can compute subtree node ids for each node in advance, but the subtree might change as we remove nodes? Actually, if we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree, so that node's descendants are all gone. But we only consider each node once in the sorted order. We can compute the full subtree at start (including all descendants) and when we remove a node, we can remove all those ids from retained set. That's fine.\n\n- For required symbols check: maintain a counter per symbol of how many nodes with that symbol are currently retained. Before removing a node, subtract the counts of symbols in the subtree. If any required symbol count goes to zero, removal invalid. After removal, update counts.\n\n- Process nodes in sorted order (descending depth, then ascending id). Iterate over sorted node id list.\n- For each node:\n  - if node id not in retained (already removed by previous step? but we process in order, children first, so if a child was removed, it won't be in retained. However, if parent is later processed, the child is already removed, so parent's child list may have been removed. So we need to check current retained children.)\n  - if node is root -> skip.\n  - if not removable -> skip.\n  - if any child is in retained set -> skip.\n  - Simulate removal: compute subtree nodes (set). Actually we can precompute subtree sets.\n  - Check if after removing subtree, all required symbols still have count >0. Use symbol counts.\n  - If valid: perform removal: remove all subtree nodes from retained set, add them to removed set (list), and update symbol counts (subtract for each node in subtree). Also record the node id (the root of the removed subtree) in removed_node_ids list in order. Note: the order of removal is the order we process nodes (deeper first). But the spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removal order is the order we remove nodes (the root nodes of the removed subtrees). For each such removal, we add the node id to removed_node_ids.\n\n- After processing all nodes, the retained set contains the IDs of nodes that are not removed. Sort retained_node_ids alphabetically (since IDs are strings), and return.\n\nEdge cases: required_symbols may include \"root\" (which is always present). Also note that if a node has a required symbol but is removable, we can still remove it if another node with that symbol remains. So we need global symbol counts.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse the case payload: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n2. Build a dict node_map: id -> node.\n3. Build children list per node: map parent_id -> list of child ids.\n4. Compute depth for each node from payload (provided). Use directly.\n5. Compute full subtree set for each node: we can do a recursive DFS from root, collecting descendant ids. But since nodes have parent, we can also compute all ancestors. Simpler: for each node, we can later find its subtree by walking down children. We can precompute all descendants set for each node using DFS and cache. Since the tree is small (7 nodes in examples), it's fine.\n6. Initialize retained = set of all node ids.\n7. Initialize symbol_counts: from all nodes, count occurrences of each symbol.\n8. Sort node ids by depth descending, then id ascending. Note: the root has depth 0, so it will be last. We skip root anyway.\n9. Iterate sorted nodes. For each node id:\n   - if node id not in retained: continue (already removed as part of subtree removal of another node).\n   - node = node_map[id]\n   - if node['id'] == 'root' or not node['removable']: continue.\n   - Get children list from node_map? Actually we need children of this node. Since we have children mapping, get child_ids = children_map.get(id, []).\n   - Check if any child_id in retained: if any, skip.\n   - Compute subtree = subtree_set[id] (precomputed). (Note: subtree includes the node itself and all descendants).\n   - Simulate symbol counts after removal: check if for any required symbol, symbol_counts[symbol] - count in subtree < 1. But careful: we need precise count of symbols in this subtree. So we can precompute symbol counts per subtree.\n   - If all required symbols will still have count >=1 after removal (i.e., current count - subtree_symbol_count[symbol] >= 1 for each required symbol), then proceed.\n   - Remove: for each id in subtree: removed set add, retained remove. Also update symbol_counts: subtract subtree_symbol_counts from global symbol_counts (or we can update as we go). But we need to update for subsequent checks. So we should update global symbol_counts by subtracting the counts from the subtree. Also add node id to removed_node_ids.\n\n10. After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained)).\n11. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nNeed to handle cases where root is not in nodes? The spec says root exists.\n\nTesting on the given fixtures:\n- We can manually simulate for the first case (a-000). But we must trust algorithm.\n\nPotential pitfalls:\n- The order of processing: depth descending, then id ascending. For nodes with same depth, id ascending.\n- Tie break: if two nodes are at same depth and both candidates, we process smaller id first. This affects removal order and possibly outcome because removing one might affect the other's removability (if they are ancestors? but they are at same depth so no ancestor relationship). They could be siblings. Removing a sibling may affect symbol counts but not the other's children status. So order may affect which gets removed if both are removable and both would satisfy required symbols. But spec says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we follow that.\n\n- The \"registered_candidate_order\" suggests we consider candidates in order of depth then id.\n\n- The \"never remove ... a node with retained children\" - we implement that.\n\n- The \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - we check.\n\n- \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" - We sort retained IDs. Removal order is the order we removed.\n\n- Also \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\" - but not needed for given examples. We can add basic validation: check that root exists, that parent references exist. But for simplicity, we can assume valid.\n\nImplementation: write solve(case) that takes a dict (the payload). Return dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe must follow strict submission: output one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing Python source code for the solve function. The function must be named solve and accept a single argument case. We'll write it as a standard Python function.\n\nWe need to escape the source code as JSON string. So we'll produce a JSON object like {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"} with proper escaping.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nWe'll use standard library only.\n\nPotential complication: The payload may have _case_nonce, but we ignore it.\n\nWe'll implement:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build structures\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        node_map[nid] = n\n        parent = n.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n    # Compute subtree and symbol counts\n    # We can compute via DFS from each node? But better to compute from bottom up: use depth sorting.\n    # We'll compute subtree_ids and symbol_counts recursively using memoization.\n    # Since no cycles, we can do post-order traversal starting from root.\n    # But we have depth available, we can sort by depth descending to ensure children processed first.\n    # Let's compute subtree_ids and subtree_symbol_counts using recursion with memoization.\n    # We'll define a function get_subtree(node_id) that recursively collects.\n    # But we need to avoid O(N^2) if done for each node. Since N is small, fine.\n    # We'll precompute subtree information for all nodes using DFS from root, but root might not be first.\n    # We'll just compute once for each node using recursion and caching.\n    \n    # Initialize sets\n    all_ids = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    # Subtree info cache\n    subtree_cache = {}\n    symbol_count_cache = {}\n    from collections import Counter\n    def compute_subtree(nid):\n        if nid in subtree_cache:\n            return\n        ids = {nid}\n        sym_count = Counter()\n        sym_count[node_map[nid]['symbol']] = 1\n        for child in children.get(nid, []):\n            compute_subtree(child)\n            ids.update(subtree_cache[child])\n            sym_count.update(symbol_count_cache[child])\n        subtree_cache[nid] = ids\n        symbol_count_cache[nid] = sym_count\n    # Need to call for all nodes, but some may not be reachable from root if tree not connected? But we assume tree.\n    # We'll compute for root first, then all nodes should be covered.\n    compute_subtree('root')  # root must exist\n    # But if there are nodes not under root? Not expected.\n    # Ensure we have computed for all nodes.\n    for nid in all_ids:\n        compute_subtree(nid)  # will use cache\n    \n    # Global symbol counts\n    global_sym_count = Counter()\n    for n in nodes:\n        global_sym_count[n['symbol']] += 1\n    \n    # Sort nodes for processing: by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    retained = set(all_ids)\n    removed_node_ids = []\n    \n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if nid == 'root' or not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Check if any child retained\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids):\n            continue\n        subtree_ids = subtree_cache[nid]\n        subtree_sym = symbol_count_cache[nid]\n        # Check required symbols\n        feasible = True\n        for req in required_symbols:\n            if global_sym_count[req] - subtree_sym.get(req, 0) == 0:\n                feasible = False\n                break\n        if not feasible:\n            continue\n        # Remove this subtree\n        for sid in subtree_ids:\n            retained.discard(sid)\n            global_sym_count.subtract(symbol_count_cache[sid])\n            # Actually subtract once overall, but careful: subtree_ids include node itself and all descendants, and we already have subtree_sym.\n        # Instead, just subtract once using subtree_sym\n        global_sym_count.subtract(subtree_sym)\n        removed_node_ids.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nBut there is a subtlety: When we remove a subtree, we need to remove all its ids from retained and also update global symbol counts accordingly. We did that. But note that when we later process other nodes (higher depth/earlier in sorted order), they may be part of a removed subtree. That's fine because we check if nid in retained.\n\nAlso note: We used compute_subtree for all nodes, but we also used global_sym_count updated after each removal. However, for subsequent nodes, the symbol_counts for the subtree of a node that has been partially removed are still based on original tree. That's a problem: If we have removed some descendants of a node (because they were removed as separate subtrees), then the subtree of the parent node as originally computed includes those descendants, but they are no longer present. However, we only consider nodes in order of descending depth, so we process deeper nodes first. By the time we consider a parent node, any deeper node that is removable and its removal does not violate constraints will have been removed. So the subtree for the parent now is smaller. But our precomputed subtree_ids includes those removed deeper nodes. That would be incorrect because we would try to remove the parent and its entire original subtree, but some of those nodes are already gone. That would cause double removal attempt and symbol count mismatch.\n\nSo the approach of precomputing subtree sets based on original tree is flawed if we remove nodes individually. Instead, we need to compute subtree dynamically or ensure that we only consider removal of entire subtrees in one shot. But the algorithm as described in the specification seems to be a single pass where we decide for each node whether to remove it (and its entire subtree) or not. But if we remove a child node first, then the parent node's subtree is smaller, but the parent may later be considered for removal. That's correct; we should consider the parent with its current remaining subtree. However, if we precompute subtree including children that are already removed, we would attempt to remove more than what is currently there.\n\nThus we need to adjust. The correct approach: process nodes bottom-up. For a node, its children have already been processed. If any child was removed, then that child is not in retained. So the node's current children (in retained) are only the ones not removed. So we can compute the current subtree of the node on the fly by traversing down from the node using the current retained set. Since the tree is small and we process each node once, we can compute the set of nodes in the current subtree (only retained nodes under the node, including the node itself). This can be done recursively, but we need to avoid O(N^2) if tree large; but with small N it's fine.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a union-find or maintain a \"parent\" pointer and when we remove a node, we mark it removed and skip.\n\nSimpler: process in sorted order, and for each node under consideration, we gather its current subtree by performing a BFS/DFS that only includes nodes that are in retained. This will automatically skip already removed nodes. This is acceptable.\n\nImplementation: for each candidate node, we compute current_subtree_ids = set([nid]) + for each child in children.get(nid, []) that is in retained, recursively collect. But since we process bottom-up, the children have already been processed and are either retained or removed. So we can just check children's retained status, and recurse only if child is retained. However, that recursion may go down multiple levels, but since children are processed earlier, their subtrees are smaller? Not necessarily, because if a child was not removed, its subtree still includes its own retained children. But we can compute recursively.\n\nWe also need the symbol counts for the current subtree. So we can compute that during the BFS.\n\nThis way, we don't need precomputed subtrees. The logic becomes:\n\n- After building children map, have a set retained initially all.\n- Sort nodes as before.\n- For each node n (in order):\n   - if nid not in retained: continue\n   - if root or not removable: continue\n   - compute current_subtree(node_id) := set of node ids in the retained tree that are descendants (including node). We can do DFS.\n   - compute current_subtree_symbols := Counter of symbols from those nodes.\n   - Check if any child of node is in retained? But we already compute subtree, so that check is redundant because if any child is retained, the subtree will include it. However, we must ensure that we don't remove a node that has retained children. So if we compute subtree and it includes children, we know children are retained. So we must not remove. So we can check if subtree size > 1 (i.e., node has any descendants retained). Actually if node has children retained, subtree includes them. So we can check if len(child_ids_retained) > 0. But better: before computing subtree, we can check if any child is in retained. If yes, skip. That's simpler and avoids heavy computation.\n\nSo algorithm:\nfor n in sorted_nodes:\n   if nid not in retained: continue\n   if nid == 'root' or not n['removable']: continue\n   child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n   if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids): continue\n   # Now compute the current subtree of this node (only retained nodes). Since children are not retained, the subtree is just {node} itself. Because children are not retained, but what about grandchildren? If a child is not retained (i.e., removed), then its subtree is gone. So indeed, if no children retained, the node has no retained descendants. So the subtree is just the node itself. That simplifies a lot! Because we process bottom-up and remove entire subtrees at once. If a child was removed, it's gone. So for a node with no retained children, its subtree is just itself. So we don't need to traverse deeper. This is correct because we only remove nodes when all their descendants have been removed (or never had any). So after processing children, if a node has no retained children, we can consider removing it. And if we remove it, its subtree (just itself) is removed.\nWait: But what about a node that has grandchildren that were removed? That would require the child to have been removed. So the node's direct children are either retained or removed. If all children are removed (not retained), then the node's subtree currently = node itself. So yes.\n\nThus the check for retained children is sufficient. So we don't need dynamic subtree computation beyond the node itself. However, we must ensure that removal of a node does not violate required symbols: that is, if we remove the node itself, does the symbol represented by that node disappear? Yes, so we just need to check if the node's symbol is required and if there is only one occurrence left. Since we are only removing one node, we can check global symbol count of that symbol minus 1. But careful: if the node has a symbol that is also present in other nodes that are still retained, that's fine.\n\nBut earlier we considered removing the entire subtree. Since we only allow removal when no children retained, the subtree is just the node. So indeed we only remove single nodes at a time. This matches the spec \"Tentatively remove a node\" as singular.\n\nNow we must ensure that when we remove a node, we also remove its descendants that are already removed? They are already removed. So it's fine.\n\nBut what about the case where a node has a child that is not removable? Then the child remains, so the node has retained child, cannot be removed. That aligns.\n\nThus the algorithm reduces to: process nodes bottom-up, for each removable node that is not root, if it has no retained children, and removing it does not eliminate any required symbol (i.e., after removal, for each required symbol, count in retained set >=1), then remove it.\n\nThis is simple. We need to maintain global symbol count of retained nodes.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- retained = set(all_ids)\n- global_sym_count = Counter of symbols from all nodes.\n- sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n- For each n in sorted_nodes:\n   nid = n['id']\n   if nid not in retained: continue\n   if nid == 'root' or not n['removable']: continue\n   child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n   if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids): continue\n   sym = n['symbol']\n   # Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to become absent.\n   # For each required symbol, we need global count after removal >=1.\n   # Since only this node's symbol decreases by 1, we only need to check its own symbol if it is required and its count equals 1.\n   # But careful: if the node's symbol is not required, no need to check.\n   # However, also if the node's symbol is required but there are multiple nodes with that symbol, safe.\n   # So we can do:\n   if sym in required_symbols and global_sym_count[sym] == 1:\n       continue   # cannot remove, would lose required symbol\n   # Actually we need to check all required symbols, but only this node's symbol changes. So check only that.\n   # But what if a required symbol is not present at all? The tree must have it initially.\n   # So condition: if sym in required_symbols and global_sym_count[sym] - 1 == 0 -> skip.\n   # Then remove:\n   retained.remove(nid)\n   global_sym_count.subtract([sym])\n   removed_node_ids.append(nid)\n\n- After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(retained).\n\nCheck with first fixture:\nnodes: root(sym root, not removable), n1(s1, not removable), n2(s2, removable), n3(s0, not removable), n4(s1, removable), n5(s2, not removable), n6(s0, removable). required_symbols: root, s1.\nProcessing order by depth desc: depth 2: n4(s1, removable), n5(s2, not removable), n6(s0, removable). Depth 1: n1(s1, not removable), n2(s2, removable), n3(s0, not removable). Depth 0: root(not removable).\nSort by id: n4, n5, n6 (depth 2). n4 id \"n4\", n5 \"n5\", n6 \"n6\". So n4 first.\nn4: retained children? n4 has no children (not in children map). So no retained children. sym = s1, required symbol, global count for s1 currently? s1 appears in n1 and n4 -> count 2. After removal, count becomes 1 which is >=1. So remove n4. removed_node_ids = ['n4']. Update: retained removes n4, global_sym_count s1 becomes 1.\nn5: not removable -> skip.\nn6: removable, no children, sym s0, not required? required_symbols are root and s1, so s0 is not required. So can remove. Remove n6. removed_node_ids = ['n4','n6'].\nNow depth 1: n1: not removable -> skip.\nn2: removable, children: n4? but n4 is removed, so no retained children. sym s2, not required. So can remove. Remove n2. removed_node_ids = ['n4','n6','n2'].\nn3: not removable -> skip.\nroot: skip.\